A||||4||G25C||You pry the dog bone back?||||1
Z||000000||G25C||10-08-1999||11:20 AM||||<BR>I just bought a ultimate dog bone.  Got <BR>it for 10 bucks out of the a fiero in <BR>the junk yard.  Getting them out is <BR>easy, but whats it take to get it to go <BR>back in?  The one end is mounted to the <BR>engine, the other end ive been able to <BR>closely pry back to the hole.  But i <BR>have a about half an inch to go.<P>Do i just need some help from another <BR>person, or is there a better way of <BR>prying on it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-08-1999||12:57 PM||||We'll assume you got the right one....... check the lenght against the old one.<P>put the car in neutral. That will take allot of the load off things.||||reg||1
Z||000002||G25C||10-08-1999||01:06 PM||||HA-AH!  I found a easy way to do it!  The length was right, but putting it in nuetral didnt seem to help much.  I just couldnt get the right amount of leverage on it....until i relized that i needed the assistance of another.<P>BUT, since there is no one else around, i used a 5 foot towing strap and hooked one end in the slot behind the dogbone mount on the engine side and brought the strap over the top and hook the other end to the bumper of my 86 4x4 chevy.  I pulled the strap slowly tight, then i just moved up a hair and PRESTO!  It went right in without damaging anything or rubbing marks into the back light panel.<P>The shifting feel is much different with this one in.  And for 10 bucks, i cant complain at all!||||reg||10
Z||000003||Phil||10-08-1999||07:19 PM||||When I put a rebuilt engine in my 85 GT I just loosened up the dog bone bracket on the engine put the bone in and tightened everything back up. No pushing or shoving.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||10-08-1999||08:08 PM||||You may want to inspect your trans mounts closely, if that much force is required to install a dog bone, one may assume that the front mount is quite likely worn/bad.<P>Mike||||reg||1
